> That extra "s" is of course strange, not the single "l".

The most likely reason I can think of for the different rules is that a
single "s" is used to indicate plurals, while a single "l" at the end of
a word has no additional special meaning.  I can't think of any specific
examples where a word ending in "-nes" or "-les" instead of "-ness" or
"-less" would be easily confused with the plural of another word, but it
does seem at least somewhat likely as a reason for the difference.  I
think I'll have to ask my friend with a Master's degree in English when
I see him tomorrow to play L5R (although I doubt he'll know why there is
a difference, either).

